{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793806031,"nonce":"GHVjVU+ql5W9Z4VI+NO3cGc5YRE=","title":"Snapshot: OSR tracing silently disables portable native code","message":"snapshot.rs portable_code_allowed permits ZYNTAX_TRACE_* and ZYNTAX_DUMP_* diagnostics, but rejects ZYNTAX_OSR_TRACE because its name does not match those prefixes. Enabling OSR transfer tracing therefore makes library helpers compile again and changes startup/promotion pressure. Confirmed on the frozen NUC binary at dfc3eda54f4e8907cb33e0fc1cd3c6de9f5129b2: plain OSR trace has no portable placements; an equivalent getenv-only diagnostic hook preserving environ restores all 119 snapshot functions. This contaminates diagnostic comparisons with normal runs.\n\nConsider an allowed ZYNTAX_TRACE_OSR alias or explicit handling, with a regression test that tracing preserves snapshot eligibility where native helper instrumentation is not required. Do not silently claim existing snapshot loops carry newly inserted transfer hooks. Evidence and scripts: /private/tmp/zyntax-method-tiers/evidence.tar.gz. No production sources modified during this investigation.","files":null}]}